{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-9:25\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"9:25 says the broad earth itself became constricted, while 5:6 denies divine intent to impose ḥaraj and gives a route to prayer when travel and absent water constrain ordinary washing.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"9:25\",\"ar\":\"وَضَاقَتْ عَلَيْكُمُ ٱلْأَرْضُ بِمَا رَحُبَتْ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"أَوْ عَلَىٰ سَفَرٍ أَوْ جَآءَ أَحَدٌۭ مِّنكُم مِّنَ ٱلْغَآئِطِ أَوْ لَٰمَسْتُمُ ٱلنِّسَآءَ فَلَمْ تَجِدُوا۟ مَآءًۭ فَتَيَمَّمُوا۟ صَعِيدًۭا طَيِّبًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ\"}],\"missing\":\"The scenes do not explicitly link Hunayn’s spatial contraction to ritual hardship. A dictionary sense for ḥaraj as constriction, or another passage joining spatial narrowness to relief, could establish whether this is more than a semantic comparison.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:28\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus describes God purifying believers and enabling prayer through alternatives; 9:28 bars the mushrikūn from the Sacred Mosque by calling them najas. Together they contrast enacted access to worship with categorical exclusion from a sanctuary.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"9:28\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّمَا ٱلْمُشْرِكُونَ نَجَسٌۭ فَلَا يَقْرَبُوا۟ ٱلْمَسْجِدَ ٱلْحَرَامَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus connects purification to approaching prayer, while 9:28 connects impurity language to approaching the Sacred Mosque.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not call the mushrikūn impure, and 9:28 does not describe the focus’s ablution or tayammum.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:32\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The same verb تَمَّ frames two divine acts: God completes His favor upon the believers in 5:6 and completes His light in 9:32 despite opposition.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"9:32\",\"ar\":\"وَيَأْبَى ٱللَّهُ إِلَّآ أَن يُتِمَّ نُورَهُۥ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages explicitly attribute completion to God; 9:32 supplies a second object for the focus’s completion verb.\",\"limits\":\"Light and favor are distinct objects, and 9:32’s opposition is not tied to the purification rules.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:84\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus has believers rise toward prayer, then wash; 9:84 uses the same standing and prayer vocabulary to forbid prayer for a dead hypocrite and standing at his grave.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"إِذَا قُمْتُمْ إِلَى ٱلصَّلَوٰةِ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"فَٱغْسِلُوا۟ وُجُوهَكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"9:84\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا تُصَلِّ عَلَىٰٓ أَحَدٍۢ مِّنْهُم مَّاتَ أَبَدًۭا وَلَا تَقُمْ عَلَىٰ قَبْرِهِۦٓ\"}],\"activation\":\"قُمْتُمْ and تَقُمْ share the standing root, and both verses name ṣalāh.\",\"limits\":\"The focus’s expression can mean setting out to pray; 9:84’s standing is at a grave, and its prohibition concerns a particular person.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:86\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:91\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Both passages use illness and ḥaraj to mark accommodation: 5:6 gives an alternative purification route when water is unavailable, while 9:91 removes hardship from the weak and ill in another duty.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَإِن كُنتُم مَّرْضَىٰٓ أَوْ عَلَىٰ سَفَرٍ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"9:91\",\"ar\":\"لَّيْسَ عَلَى ٱلضُّعَفَآءِ وَلَا عَلَى ٱلْمَرْضَىٰ وَلَا عَلَى ٱلَّذِينَ لَا يَجِدُونَ مَا يُنفِقُونَ حَرَجٌ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared terms المرضى and حرج put the focus’s purification concession within a broader pattern of duties adjusted for incapacity.\",\"limits\":\"9:91 concerns participation in fighting and does not specify ritual purity or water.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:108\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus presents purification as God’s purpose for believers; 9:108 describes men who love to purify themselves and whom God loves. The related root makes the rite resonate with a desired practice and valued disposition.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلَٰكِن يُرِيدُ لِيُطَهِّرَكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"9:108\",\"ar\":\"فِيهِ رِجَالٌۭ يُحِبُّونَ أَن يَتَطَهَّرُوا۟ ۚ وَٱللَّهُ يُحِبُّ ٱلْمُطَّهِّرِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The repeated ط هـ ر root and the focus’s prayer setting meet 9:108’s mosque and purification language.\",\"limits\":\"9:108 does not identify its purification with the washing or tayammum procedure in 5:6.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:128\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus denies that God intends ḥaraj; 9:128 describes the Messenger as deeply affected by what burdens the people and compassionate toward believers. The two passages align divine accommodation with prophetic concern for hardship.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"9:128\",\"ar\":\"عَزِيزٌ عَلَيْهِ مَا عَنِتُّمْ حَرِيصٌ عَلَيْكُم بِٱلْمُؤْمِنِينَ رَءُوفٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s no-ḥaraj rationale is paired with 9:128’s explicit concern over what burdens the community.\",\"limits\":\"The verses use different words for hardship, and 9:128 does not mention ritual law.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:51\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:60\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus links completion of God’s favor to gratitude; 10:60 sets God’s favor against people who fail to give thanks, making ingratitude a counter-response to the outcome the focus invites.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"10:60\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَذُو فَضْلٍ عَلَى ٱلنَّاسِ وَلَٰكِنَّ أَكْثَرَهُمْ لَا يَشْكُرُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages connect divine beneficence with the ش ك ر root, though one invites thanks and the other names its absence.\",\"limits\":\"10:60 speaks of God’s فضل generally, not specifically the purification rite or its completion.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:109\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-11:119\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The root ت م م does not itself signal a favorable outcome: in 11:119 it describes completion of a decree to fill Hell, while 5:6 specifies that what God completes is His favor.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"11:119\",\"ar\":\"وَتَمَّتْ كَلِمَةُ رَبِّكَ لَأَمْلَأَنَّ جَهَنَّمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared completion root invites a contrast, while the focus’s object نعمتَه supplies its positive direction.\",\"limits\":\"The verses concern different outcomes and do not imply that the completion in 5:6 has the same setting or referent as the decree in 11:119.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:6\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The formula يُتِمُّ نِعْمَتَهُ recurs in 12:6: God completes His favor upon Joseph and Jacob’s family through selection and teaching. This lets the focus’s favor be heard as something unfolding through guidance, not just a single benefit.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"12:6\",\"ar\":\"وَيُتِمُّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكَ وَعَلَىٰٓ ءَالِ يَعْقُوبَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same completion verb, favor noun, and divine subject appear in both passages.\",\"limits\":\"12:6 addresses Joseph and his family in a narrative of selection and teaching, not the community’s purification practice.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:9\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The focus uses وُجُوهَكُمْ for bodily faces to be washed; 12:9 uses the same word in an idiom about securing the father’s regard. The Qur’anic usage allows a relational sense of wajh alongside the focus’s bodily one.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"فَٱغْسِلُوا۟ وُجُوهَكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"12:9\",\"ar\":\"يَخْلُ لَكُمْ وَجْهُ أَبِيكُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared word وجه makes the focus’s bodily object audible against a Qur’anic idiom of regard.\",\"limits\":\"The focus’s washing construction establishes a bodily meaning here; 12:9 does not describe washing or purification.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:30\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"12:30 depicts a woman’s erotic pursuit, while 5:6 uses لَامَسْتُمُ ٱلنِّسَاءَ as a condition in the purification passage. The scene may be relevant to the sexual range of that expression, but it describes desire rather than actual touch.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"أَوْ لَٰمَسْتُمُ ٱلنِّسَآءَ\"},{\"ref\":\"12:30\",\"ar\":\"ٱمْرَأَتُ ٱلْعَزِيزِ تُرَٰوِدُ فَتَىٰهَا عَن نَّفْسِهِۦ\"}],\"missing\":\"12:30 has no لمس wording or completed contact. A Qur’anic occurrence of لامس in an explicitly sexual scene, or a dictionary sense that clarifies the verb’s range, could decide whether this parallel informs the focus’s expression.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:38\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Joseph calls his faith and knowledge God’s فضل and says most people do not give thanks; 5:6 similarly presents purification as completion of divine favor with gratitude as its hoped-for response.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"12:38\",\"ar\":\"ذَٰلِكَ مِن فَضْلِ ٱللَّهِ عَلَيْنَا وَعَلَى ٱلنَّاسِ وَلَٰكِنَّ أَكْثَرَ ٱلنَّاسِ لَا يَشْكُرُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages join divine favor to the ش ك ر root, with Joseph’s account locating favor in guidance and knowledge.\",\"limits\":\"12:38 does not mention purification, and its فضل is not explicitly named نعمة.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:5\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"14:5 names the صَبَّار شَكُور as a recipient of signs, placing gratitude among responses to divine guidance. Its link to the focus is the gratitude outcome, not the rite itself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:5\",\"ar\":\"لَءَايَٰتٍۢ لِّكُلِّ صَبَّارٍۢ شَكُورٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared ش ك ر root connects the focus’s hoped-for communal response with 14:5’s description of a grateful person.\",\"limits\":\"14:5 pairs gratitude with patience and says nothing about prayer preparation or purification.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:7\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"14:6–7 moves from remembering a concrete saving favor to the promise of increase for gratitude. Beside 5:6, this frames gratitude as a response to favor that may itself open into further increase.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱذْكُرُوا۟ نِعْمَةَ ٱللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ إِذْ أَنجَىٰكُم مِّنْ ءَالِ فِرْعَوْنَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:7\",\"ar\":\"لَئِن شَكَرْتُمْ لَأَزِيدَنَّكُمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names completion of favor and gratitude; the adjacent 14:6–7 verses explicitly sequence favor, rescue, gratitude, and increase.\",\"limits\":\"14:6–7 addresses Moses’s people and does not identify purification as the favor or specify the focus’s promised outcome.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:28\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"14:28 supplies the opposite response to the focus’s hoped-for gratitude: people replace God’s favor with disbelief. The contrast makes recognition and preservation of favor part of what gratitude entails.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:28\",\"ar\":\"بَدَّلُوا۟ نِعْمَتَ ٱللَّهِ كُفْرًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages name divine niʿma; one points toward thanks, the other toward its reversal.\",\"limits\":\"14:28 does not identify its favor with the purification instruction in 5:6.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:37\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"14:37 places prayer in a valley without cultivation and asks for provision so its people may give thanks. Beside 5:6, this makes prayer under material constraint a setting in which divine provision and the gratitude it enables matter.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"أَوْ عَلَىٰ سَفَرٍ أَوْ جَآءَ أَحَدٌۭ مِّنكُم مِّنَ ٱلْغَآئِطِ أَوْ لَٰمَسْتُمُ ٱلنِّسَآءَ فَلَمْ تَجِدُوا۟ مَآءًۭ فَتَيَمَّمُوا۟ صَعِيدًۭا طَيِّبًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"14:37\",\"ar\":\"بِوَادٍ غَيْرِ ذِى زَرْعٍ عِندَ بَيْتِكَ ٱلْمُحَرَّمِ رَبَّنَا لِيُقِيمُوا۟ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:37\",\"ar\":\"وَٱرْزُقْهُم مِّنَ ٱلثَّمَرَٰتِ لَعَلَّهُمْ يَشْكُرُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages join prayer with conditions affecting provision and end in gratitude.\",\"limits\":\"14:37 says the valley lacks cultivation, not that water is absent; it does not mention tayammum or purification.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:50\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus has faces washed before prayer; 14:50 has fire engulfing faces in the scene of judgment. This stark bodily contrast makes the face a site where a person’s condition is made visible.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"فَٱغْسِلُوا۟ وُجُوهَكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:50\",\"ar\":\"وَتَغْشَىٰ وُجُوهَهُمُ ٱلنَّارُ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages name faces as the direct site of a bodily action, one washing and the other engulfment by fire.\",\"limits\":\"14:50 does not mention prayer or purification, so the contrast is a reading prompted by the shared word and bodily scene.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:61\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:65\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:7\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"16:7 depicts a journey that would be reached only with great strain, then names God as compassionate; 5:6 denies intent to impose ḥaraj and adjusts purification when travel or water scarcity intervenes. Both portray divine provision answering human difficulty.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"مَا يُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ لِيَجْعَلَ عَلَيْكُم مِّنْ حَرَجٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"أَوْ عَلَىٰ سَفَرٍ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:7\",\"ar\":\"لَّمْ تَكُونُوا۟ بَٰلِغِيهِ إِلَّا بِشِقِّ ٱلْأَنفُسِ ۚ إِنَّ رَبَّكُمْ لَرَءُوفٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"Travel and hardship are explicit in both passages, and each associates relief with divine compassion.\",\"limits\":\"16:7 concerns transport by livestock, not ritual law or the focus’s specific no-water condition.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:10\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"16:10 presents rainwater as drink and as support for grazing; 5:6 gives water a distinct ritual use in washing and provides earth when that medium is unavailable.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"فَٱغْسِلُوا۟ وُجُوهَكُمْ وَأَيْدِيَكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:10\",\"ar\":\"أَنزَلَ مِنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ مَآءًۭ ۖ لَّكُم مِّنْهُ شَرَابٌۭ وَمِنْهُ شَجَرٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared water term contrasts bodily purification with nourishment and vegetation as uses of divine provision.\",\"limits\":\"16:10 neither mentions purification nor establishes that its water is the focus’s ritual medium.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:14\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"16:14 ends the account of sea benefits with لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ, the same closing formula as the focus; gratitude follows both natural provision and the purification rule.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:14\",\"ar\":\"وَلِتَبْتَغُوا۟ مِن فَضْلِهِۦ وَلَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The repeated wording and the mention of divine فضل in 16:14 place the focus’s ending in a broader formula of gratitude for divine benefits.\",\"limits\":\"16:14’s sea benefits do not explain the rite or its conditions.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:18\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"16:18 says God’s favor cannot be counted, while 5:6 speaks of completing His favor. The contrast allows this particular favor to sit within a blessing too vast to enumerate.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:18\",\"ar\":\"وَإِن تَعُدُّوا۟ نِعْمَةَ ٱللَّهِ لَا تُحْصُوهَا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages use نعمة; the focus’s completion language meets 16:18’s image of uncountable favor.\",\"limits\":\"16:18 does not identify the purification rule as one of those blessings or explain what completion means in 5:6.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:54\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:61\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:65\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"16:65 pairs water from the sky with the earth’s revival; 5:6 uses clean earth for purification when water cannot be found. This could make the earth substitute resonate with renewal rather than mere lack.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"فَتَيَمَّمُوا۟ صَعِيدًۭا طَيِّبًۭا فَٱمْسَحُوا۟ بِوُجُوهِكُمْ وَأَيْدِيكُم مِّنْهُ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:65\",\"ar\":\"أَنزَلَ مِنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ مَآءًۭ فَأَحْيَا بِهِ ٱلْأَرْضَ بَعْدَ مَوْتِهَآ\"}],\"missing\":\"16:65 does not mention purification or tayammum, and 5:6 does not describe earth as reviving. Another passage linking ritual earth with life or renewal, or a lexical sense that connects ṣaʿīd ṭayyib to this scene, could establish the proposed resonance.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:78\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"16:78 calls hearing, sight, and hearts gifts that invite gratitude; 5:6 likewise ends in gratitude while directing attention to the face and hands as bodily sites of purification.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وُجُوهَكُمْ وَأَيْدِيَكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:78\",\"ar\":\"وَجَعَلَ لَكُمُ ٱلسَّمْعَ وَٱلْأَبْصَٰرَ وَٱلْأَفْـِٔدَةَ ۙ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages connect bodily endowments or bodily practice with the same gratitude formula.\",\"limits\":\"16:78 does not mention washing, and 5:6 does not identify purification as thanks for the senses.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:81\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"16:81 repeats the focus’s formula that God completes His favor upon the community, after listing shelter and clothing that protect them. Its different ending, “so that you may submit,” shows that the formula can gather practical provision and religious guidance toward distinct responses.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"وَلِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:81\",\"ar\":\"كَذَٰلِكَ يُتِمُّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُسْلِمُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The phrase يُتِمُّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ recurs almost exactly, while both passages follow it with a لَعَلَّكُمْ purpose clause.\",\"limits\":\"16:81’s examples are shelter and protection, not purification; submission and gratitude should not be treated as identical outcomes.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:83\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"16:83 describes people who recognize God’s favor and then deny it, a counterexample to the gratitude that 5:6 hopes will follow completion of favor.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"لِيُتِمَّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:83\",\"ar\":\"يَعْرِفُونَ نِعْمَتَ ٱللَّهِ ثُمَّ يُنكِرُونَهَا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages name God’s favor, while the focus’s gratitude outcome meets 16:83’s movement from recognition to denial.\",\"limits\":\"16:83 does not specify the favor as purification or identify its deniers with the focus’s addressees.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:114\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"16:114 pairs طَيِّبًا provision with a command to thank God for His favor; 5:6 describes clean earth as طَيِّبًا and ends by hoping for thanks. This places tayammum’s medium within a broader language of good provision and gratitude.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"فَتَيَمَّمُوا۟ صَعِيدًۭا طَيِّبًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:114\",\"ar\":\"فَكُلُوا۟ مِمَّا رَزَقَكُمُ ٱللَّهُ حَلَٰلًۭا طَيِّبًۭا وَٱشْكُرُوا۟ نِعْمَتَ ٱللَّهِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared ṭayyib wording and gratitude language occur together in both passages, with 16:114 explicitly naming God’s favor.\",\"limits\":\"16:114 describes food; it does not establish that ṭayyib has precisely the same legal or material sense for earth in 5:6.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:121\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"16:121 characterizes Abraham as grateful for God’s blessings; the focus’s لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ frames gratitude as the hoped-for response of the addressed community.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:121\",\"ar\":\"شَاكِرًۭا لِّأَنْعُمِهِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both use the ش ك ر root in response to divine favor, though one describes an exemplary person and the other addresses a community.\",\"limits\":\"16:121 does not mention purification or the focus’s practical concessions.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:3\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"17:3 calls Noah a grateful servant, offering an individual model beside the focus’s hope that the community will be grateful.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"لَعَلَّكُمْ تَشْكُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"17:3\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّهُۥ كَانَ عَبْدًۭا شَكُورًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared gratitude root links the focus’s invited response with a Quranic description of a servant’s enduring quality.\",\"limits\":\"17:3 gives no purification, prayer, or favor wording to connect that model to the rite.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:7\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus has faces washed in preparation for prayer; 17:7 says faces will be made wretched in a scene that also names entry into the mosque. Their juxtaposition makes the face a visible register of a person’s relation to worship and consequence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"5:6\",\"ar\":\"فَٱغْسِلُوا۟ وُجُوهَكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"17:7\",\"ar\":\"لِيَسُۥٓـُٔوا۟ وُجُوهَكُمْ وَلِيَدْخُلُوا۟ ٱلْمَسْجِدَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages name faces, and 17:7 places its face language alongside entry into a mosque, while the focus prepares people for prayer.\",\"limits\":\"17:7 does not mention ablution or prayer; the contrast rests on shared bodily and worship-space imagery.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:23\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-17:68\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}"}}
